{"name":"Bold Eagle","hex":"#463d2f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Courageous; daring.","Visually striking; conspicuous."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make (a font or some text) bold.","To make bold or daring."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bold"},{"term":"eagle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several large carnivorous and carrion-eating birds in the family Accipitridae, having a powerful hooked bill and keen vision.","A gold coin with a face value of ten dollars, formerly used in the United States."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To score an eagle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eagle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eagle","blocks":[{"text":"Eagle is the common name for certain large predatory birds of prey within the family of the Accipitridae. While on a genetic level, only the subfamily Aquilinae comprises \"true eagles\", many other species are commonly referred to as eagles, such as the bald eagle, and the term generally carries no taxonomic weight. Most of the 68 species of eagles are from Eurasia and Africa. Outside this area, just 14 species can be found—two in North America, nine in Central and South America, and three in Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Eagles_together.jpg/330px-Eagles_together.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Old_Abe%2C_the_live_war_eagle_of_Wisconsin%2C_from_the_Centennial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Old_Abe%2C_the_live_war_eagle_of_Wisconsin%2C_from_the_Centennial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"An Eagle named in ho